WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:04.292
- What's up, believe nation?

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:06.747
I started the Did You Know
series to learn a little bit

00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:09.046
more about some of our
famous entrepreneurs,

00:00:09.046 --> 00:00:11.789
our favorite ones, to
learn what makes them tick,

00:00:11.789 --> 00:00:13.378
to find a couple of surprising facts

00:00:13.378 --> 00:00:15.536
that maybe you haven't heard before.

00:00:15.536 --> 00:00:18.485
And so, today we're going to
learn nine surprising facts

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:19.818
about Elon Musk.

00:00:20.908 --> 00:00:23.825
(electronic music)

00:00:31.841 --> 00:00:32.792
(bell rings)

00:00:32.792 --> 00:00:34.443
Fact number one, he almost went broke

00:00:34.443 --> 00:00:36.665
trying to save Space X and Tesla.

00:00:36.665 --> 00:00:38.622
We used up all the,

00:00:38.622 --> 00:00:42.789
invested 100 million, then
still needed more money.

00:00:44.852 --> 00:00:49.233
And then there was a big
recession of 2008 and '09

00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:51.357
and in the end, I had
to invest everything.

00:00:51.357 --> 00:00:53.852
And I actually was
borrowing money from friends

00:00:53.852 --> 00:00:54.807
to pay the rent.

00:00:54.807 --> 00:00:57.552
- You were on the edge of actually

00:00:57.552 --> 00:01:00.406
bankruptcy.
- I didn't even own a house.

00:01:00.406 --> 00:01:01.768
- [Man] You could have bought an island.

00:01:01.768 --> 00:01:02.601
- Yes.

00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:03.979
- And you could have sipped cocktails

00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:05.926
for the rest of your life.

00:01:05.926 --> 00:01:08.862
Why did you want to
take the risks involved

00:01:08.862 --> 00:01:12.197
in these businesses
that you've gone in to?

00:01:12.197 --> 00:01:16.255
- Well, I have to say
at times I've wondered.

00:01:16.255 --> 00:01:20.422
(laughs) I wondered if it
was really the right choice.

00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:26.214
But it has actually been
a very difficult journey,

00:01:26.214 --> 00:01:27.381
I have to say.

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:33.682
But I think, there's
certain important things

00:01:33.682 --> 00:01:37.849
that we must do in order
for the future to be good.

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:40.797
We must have sustainable energy.

00:01:40.797 --> 00:01:42.971
If we don't have that, the
future's going to be terrible.

00:01:42.971 --> 00:01:44.463
- Were you a little
naive when you thought,

00:01:44.463 --> 00:01:48.859
I can easily build an
electric car in a rocket?

00:01:48.859 --> 00:01:52.179
- I didn't think it would be easy.

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:54.932
Like, I said, I thought
they would probably fail.

00:01:54.932 --> 00:01:59.652
But creating a company is
almost like having child.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:02.547
So it's sort of like,
how do you say your child

00:02:02.547 --> 00:02:04.297
should not have food?

00:02:06.165 --> 00:02:08.948
- So once you have the
company, you have to feed it

00:02:08.948 --> 00:02:12.402
and nurse it and take care of it,

00:02:12.402 --> 00:02:14.761
even if it ruins you.

00:02:14.761 --> 00:02:15.594
- Yeah.

00:02:18.148 --> 00:02:21.565
- But there was some tough times in 2008.

00:02:24.781 --> 00:02:25.781
End of 2008.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.513
How did you get through
that period of crisis?

00:02:30.513 --> 00:02:31.346
- Yeah.

00:02:33.708 --> 00:02:34.841
Could we just break for a second?

00:02:34.841 --> 00:02:35.674
- [Man] Sure, sure, sure.

00:02:35.674 --> 00:02:37.473
Of course, yeah, yeah.

00:02:37.473 --> 00:02:40.385
- Did you think, I need to pack this in?

00:02:40.385 --> 00:02:41.218
- Never.

00:02:42.096 --> 00:02:43.365
- Why not?

00:02:43.365 --> 00:02:45.473
- I don't ever give up.

00:02:45.473 --> 00:02:49.908
I mean, I'd have to be dead
or completely incapacitated.

00:02:49.908 --> 00:02:51.158
(bell rings)

00:02:51.158 --> 00:02:54.689
- Fact number two, Steve
Jobs was rude to him.

00:02:54.689 --> 00:02:58.856
- Steve Jobs was and is a
wonderful Silicon Valley icon.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:01.938
Is he someone that you've admired

00:03:01.938 --> 00:03:05.979
and what have you learned
from Steve's life and work?

00:03:05.979 --> 00:03:10.360
- Well, he's certainly
someone I've admired.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.946
Although I did try to talk
to him once at a party,

00:03:11.946 --> 00:03:13.819
and he was super rude to me.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:16.024
(host and audience laugh)

00:03:16.024 --> 00:03:17.064
But I don't think it was me.

00:03:17.064 --> 00:03:19.057
I think it was par for the course.

00:03:19.057 --> 00:03:20.228
- [Host] I think you
weren't the first one.

00:03:20.228 --> 00:03:22.395
- Yeah, not the first, no.

00:03:23.645 --> 00:03:25.752
But, yeah, it's actually,

00:03:25.752 --> 00:03:27.382
Larry Page is an old friend of mine.

00:03:27.382 --> 00:03:30.219
I've known since before he got
venture funding for Google.

00:03:30.219 --> 00:03:31.946
And Larry was the guy that
introduced me to Steve Jobs.

00:03:31.946 --> 00:03:34.611
So it's not like I'm going
and tugging on his coat,

00:03:34.611 --> 00:03:36.640
like, please talk to me.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.948
(host and audience laugh)

00:03:38.948 --> 00:03:42.278
So, I was introduced by Larry
Page, that's not bad, so.

00:03:42.278 --> 00:03:44.099
But, I mean, obviously
he was an incredible guy

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:46.849
and made fantastic products that,

00:03:48.142 --> 00:03:50.142
and there was a certain,

00:03:51.813 --> 00:03:53.305
the guy had a certain magic about him.

00:03:53.305 --> 00:03:56.078
That was really inspiring.

00:03:56.078 --> 00:03:58.867
So I think that's really great.

00:03:58.867 --> 00:04:02.563
- Is it that magic that
you try and emulate?

00:04:02.563 --> 00:04:06.312
- No, I think Steve Jobs is
way cooler than I am, so.

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:07.600
(bell rings)

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.271
- Fact number three, he once shredded

00:04:10.271 --> 00:04:13.872
a $1 million uninsured F1 McLaren.

00:04:13.872 --> 00:04:15.875
- Not really, no,
actually, and I never did.

00:04:15.875 --> 00:04:17.318
Although that was one of
the things that was sort

00:04:17.318 --> 00:04:19.290
of talked about at one
point, but I never did.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:21.849
In fact, I've never raced
anyone with the MClaren.

00:04:21.849 --> 00:04:23.019
I had it for several years.

00:04:23.019 --> 00:04:25.331
I put 11,000 miles on it,
and I drove it from L.A.

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:27.135
to San Francisco, and
it was my daily driver,

00:04:27.135 --> 00:04:28.797
which is crazy car to
have as a daily driver,

00:04:28.797 --> 00:04:30.340
particularly on the 405.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:33.310
- So, but I understand when you got it--

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:34.239
- [Elon] Yeah.

00:04:34.239 --> 00:04:36.030
- You're driving down 280

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.484
and you wrecked it.

00:04:37.484 --> 00:04:38.487
- [Elon] No, well--

00:04:38.487 --> 00:04:40.278
- Let me just tell the story,
and then you can correct it.

00:04:40.278 --> 00:04:41.111
- All right.
(audience laughs)

00:04:41.111 --> 00:04:42.594
- 'Cause the story's great,
and I hope it's true.

00:04:42.594 --> 00:04:43.782
- Yeah, let's see if
the story that you tell

00:04:43.782 --> 00:04:46.586
is actually how that
compares to the reality,

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:48.143
'cause the reality is pretty messed up.

00:04:48.143 --> 00:04:49.504
(audience laughs)

00:04:49.504 --> 00:04:50.720
- Hopefully the reality's better.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.133
So, you wreck the car.

00:04:52.133 --> 00:04:53.146
You get out of the car.

00:04:53.146 --> 00:04:55.046
You're doubling over with laughter.

00:04:55.046 --> 00:04:55.879
- [Elon] (laughs) Oh, really?

00:04:55.879 --> 00:04:57.277
- And the person with you said,

00:04:57.277 --> 00:04:59.645
"Why are you laughing that
you just wrecked this car?"

00:04:59.645 --> 00:05:01.478
And you said, "No, you
don't know the funny part.

00:05:01.478 --> 00:05:02.917
"It wasn't insured."

00:05:02.917 --> 00:05:05.421
(both laughing)

00:05:05.421 --> 00:05:08.054
- Well, the punch line's correct.

00:05:08.054 --> 00:05:10.721
(both laughing)

00:05:12.922 --> 00:05:17.405
Yeah, so I was actually, I
was driving up Sand Hill Road

00:05:17.405 --> 00:05:20.265
with Peter Thiel, one of
the co-founder's of PayPal.

00:05:20.265 --> 00:05:23.016
We're actually driving
to see Michael Rits.

00:05:23.016 --> 00:05:24.349
This is in 2000.

00:05:25.501 --> 00:05:27.162
And so we're driving up Sand Hill Road.

00:05:27.162 --> 00:05:29.210
Didn't really know how to McLaren.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:34.032
And Peter says, "So, what
can this do?" (laughs)

00:05:34.032 --> 00:05:36.194
And then I'm probably number one

00:05:36.194 --> 00:05:38.148
on the list of famous last words.

00:05:38.148 --> 00:05:39.778
I said, "Watch this."

00:05:39.778 --> 00:05:43.198
(both laughing)

00:05:43.198 --> 00:05:47.106
So I floored and did a
lane change on Sand Hill.

00:05:47.106 --> 00:05:50.755
And the McLaren has no
traction control or anything.

00:05:50.755 --> 00:05:53.632
It's just massive power to the wheels.

00:05:53.632 --> 00:05:58.130
So 640 brake horsepower
and it only ways a ton.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:05:59.601
So it has massive power to weight.

00:05:59.601 --> 00:06:02.010
It can break wheels free
at 80 miles an hour.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:06.177
So I broke the rear end
free and started spinning.

00:06:07.017 --> 00:06:08.184
And I sort of,

00:06:09.258 --> 00:06:10.591
let's see, I think it was sort of,

00:06:10.591 --> 00:06:13.674
I was going straight and then turned.

00:06:14.723 --> 00:06:17.580
And I remember seeing the
cars coming towards me

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.161
while I was going backwards.

00:06:19.161 --> 00:06:21.750
And then we hit an embankment,

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.477
sort of a 45 degree
embankment on Sand Hill,

00:06:24.477 --> 00:06:26.781
which tossed the car into
the air like a discus,

00:06:26.781 --> 00:06:30.515
and it kept rotating with about
three foot of air clearance

00:06:30.515 --> 00:06:32.279
according to witnesses.

00:06:32.279 --> 00:06:33.655
(audience and host laugh)

00:06:33.655 --> 00:06:35.573
And then slammed down on the ground

00:06:35.573 --> 00:06:37.990
going the original direction.

00:06:38.927 --> 00:06:41.510
And it blew the suspension out.

00:06:42.371 --> 00:06:43.591
Now, it didn't actually wreck the car.

00:06:43.591 --> 00:06:46.048
The core which chassis
and the engine were okay.

00:06:46.048 --> 00:06:47.165
- Thank God.

00:06:47.165 --> 00:06:48.874
- But all the glass and the wheels

00:06:48.874 --> 00:06:50.329
and everything was shredded.

00:06:50.329 --> 00:06:55.195
And there was massive body
damage to the front and rear.

00:06:55.195 --> 00:06:56.028
And, yeah.

00:06:57.969 --> 00:06:59.213
(bell rings)

00:06:59.213 --> 00:07:00.165
- Fact number four.

00:07:00.165 --> 00:07:02.525
He has an idea for aal
fifth mode of transport,

00:07:02.525 --> 00:07:03.899
the hyperloop.

00:07:03.899 --> 00:07:05.627
- I know that there are various
companies that are trying

00:07:05.627 --> 00:07:08.116
to create the hyperloop.

00:07:08.116 --> 00:07:12.214
And honestly I think it's a
lot easier than people think.

00:07:12.214 --> 00:07:14.405
- The blueprints are pretty complicated.

00:07:14.405 --> 00:07:17.265
- Well, blueprints are
always kind of complicated,

00:07:17.265 --> 00:07:19.464
and, I mean, yes, there's math.

00:07:19.464 --> 00:07:21.213
But it's really not that hard.

00:07:21.213 --> 00:07:23.669
- It still sounds pretty
complicated, Elon.

00:07:23.669 --> 00:07:26.207
- It's like a tube with
an air hockey table.

00:07:26.207 --> 00:07:30.040
It's just a low-pressure
tube with a pod in it

00:07:31.108 --> 00:07:34.358
that runs on air bearings, on air skis,

00:07:35.403 --> 00:07:38.088
with an with an air
compressor on the front

00:07:38.088 --> 00:07:41.155
that's taking the high pressure
air build-up on the nose

00:07:41.155 --> 00:07:43.859
and pumping it through the air skis.

00:07:43.859 --> 00:07:46.873
It's really, I swear, it's not that hard.

00:07:46.873 --> 00:07:47.921
(bell rings)

00:07:47.921 --> 00:07:49.050
- Fact number five,

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.926
he considered buying intercontinental

00:07:50.926 --> 00:07:53.089
ballistic missiles from Russia.

00:07:53.089 --> 00:07:54.094
- Speaking of crazy things.

00:07:54.094 --> 00:07:57.654
Went to Russia three
times to negotiate a deal

00:07:57.654 --> 00:08:01.778
to buy a couple of the largest
ICBMs in the Russian fleet.

00:08:01.778 --> 00:08:04.030
(host and audience laugh)

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:05.355
A strange experience.

00:08:05.355 --> 00:08:09.545
- (laughs) How do you even
get into that negotiation?

00:08:09.545 --> 00:08:12.442
- Well, you talk to
people who know people,

00:08:12.442 --> 00:08:16.210
and pretty soon you're talking
to the Russian rocket forces,

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:17.043
and they,

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:23.615
turns out Russia's quite
a capitalist society.

00:08:23.615 --> 00:08:25.982
(host and audience laugh)

00:08:25.982 --> 00:08:26.815
And,

00:08:27.691 --> 00:08:31.526
but I've definitely
had some weird meetings

00:08:31.526 --> 00:08:35.422
in places I swear that look
like a sanitarium or something,

00:08:35.422 --> 00:08:36.540
I don't know.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:37.574
It was very odd.

00:08:37.574 --> 00:08:39.444
- We keep coming back
to the theme of crazy.

00:08:39.444 --> 00:08:43.285
- Yeah, seriously, this
place had padded walls.

00:08:43.285 --> 00:08:44.954
(both laughing)

00:08:44.954 --> 00:08:47.371
Why do you have padded walls?

00:08:49.092 --> 00:08:50.175
It was weird.

00:08:51.471 --> 00:08:55.670
And, yeah, then I had
some sort of Russian guy

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:58.430
who was missing a front
tooth started yelling at me.

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:00.965
(laughs) Because one of his
front teeth was missing,

00:09:00.965 --> 00:09:04.329
it was like spit flying at me
in a place with padded walls.

00:09:04.329 --> 00:09:06.309
I'm like this is really bizarre.

00:09:06.309 --> 00:09:07.181
- What happens to my life?

00:09:07.181 --> 00:09:08.014
- Right.

00:09:08.014 --> 00:09:08.847
- And that was the
moment when you thought,

00:09:08.847 --> 00:09:10.047
I'm going to build my own rocket?

00:09:10.047 --> 00:09:11.192
- Yeah.

00:09:11.192 --> 00:09:12.058
(bell rings)

00:09:12.058 --> 00:09:13.222
- Fact number six.

00:09:13.222 --> 00:09:15.262
Reading "The Hitchhiker's
Guide to the Galaxy"

00:09:15.262 --> 00:09:17.738
was a major turning point in his life.

00:09:17.738 --> 00:09:19.494
- Basically, I sort of
was thinking about this

00:09:19.494 --> 00:09:21.948
when I was bored as a
kid, and trying to find

00:09:21.948 --> 00:09:26.073
some meaning in life, like
what's the meaning of life?

00:09:26.073 --> 00:09:29.813
And I got quite sort of
sad about it, actually,

00:09:29.813 --> 00:09:31.199
when I was a teenager.

00:09:31.199 --> 00:09:33.877
And then I read the "Hitchhiker's
Guide to the Galaxy".

00:09:33.877 --> 00:09:36.140
(audience laughs)

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.640
Which basically said the real,

00:09:39.746 --> 00:09:41.617
basically the universe is the answer.

00:09:41.617 --> 00:09:43.736
And you just need to
figure out the questions.

00:09:43.736 --> 00:09:44.653
So I think,

00:09:46.296 --> 00:09:50.948
that's, if I can help kind
of figure out the questions,

00:09:50.948 --> 00:09:54.281
then I'd consider that to be meaningful.

00:09:56.379 --> 00:09:59.911
Maybe for the name of the first trip

00:09:59.911 --> 00:10:02.379
that goes to Mars, not
the name of the whole,

00:10:02.379 --> 00:10:05.538
sort of the ship line or
whatever, necessarily,

00:10:05.538 --> 00:10:07.077
but the first trip that goes to Mars,

00:10:07.077 --> 00:10:09.916
my current favorite is Heart of Gold,

00:10:09.916 --> 00:10:12.736
from The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy.

00:10:12.736 --> 00:10:15.348
(audience claps and cheers)

00:10:15.348 --> 00:10:17.662
So I think probably
we'll name the first ship

00:10:17.662 --> 00:10:19.457
that goes to mars Heart of Gold,

00:10:19.457 --> 00:10:23.457
and I like the fact that
it's driven by infinite

00:10:25.685 --> 00:10:28.852
improbability because I think our trip

00:10:28.852 --> 00:10:31.271
is also extremely impossible.

00:10:31.271 --> 00:10:34.938
So, I like the infinite
improbability drive.

00:10:36.196 --> 00:10:37.467
(bell rings)

00:10:37.467 --> 00:10:40.114
- Fact number seven, he taught
himself computer programming

00:10:40.114 --> 00:10:42.730
at a young age and sold his first program

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.397
Blast Star for $500.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:48.341
- I was went into a store in South Africa

00:10:48.341 --> 00:10:50.591
and saw a Commodore VIC-20.

00:10:51.762 --> 00:10:52.595
And

00:10:54.162 --> 00:10:55.486
I think I was nine
years old, I don't know,

00:10:55.486 --> 00:10:57.116
somewhere around that time.

00:10:57.116 --> 00:11:00.053
And I thought this was
the most awesome thing

00:11:00.053 --> 00:11:01.185
I had ever seen.

00:11:01.185 --> 00:11:04.656
And you could write computer
programs and make games

00:11:04.656 --> 00:11:08.322
and I played Atari and other things,

00:11:08.322 --> 00:11:09.897
other game consoles

00:11:09.897 --> 00:11:12.814
from when I was maybe six or seven.

00:11:13.652 --> 00:11:15.252
So the idea of being able to create games

00:11:15.252 --> 00:11:16.796
I thought was really exciting.

00:11:16.796 --> 00:11:20.013
I taught myself how to program
computers when I was a kid,

00:11:20.013 --> 00:11:22.868
and bought my first
computer when I was 10,

00:11:22.868 --> 00:11:25.355
and sold my first commercial
program when I was 12.

00:11:25.355 --> 00:11:26.323
(bell rings)

00:11:26.323 --> 00:11:28.951
Fact number eight, he
believes that we're possibly

00:11:28.951 --> 00:11:30.846
living in a simulation.

00:11:30.846 --> 00:11:34.212
The strongest argument for
us being in a simulation,

00:11:34.212 --> 00:11:38.379
probably being in a simulation,
I think is the following.

00:11:39.224 --> 00:11:41.391
40 years ago, we had pong.

00:11:42.747 --> 00:11:44.924
Like two rectangles and a dot.

00:11:44.924 --> 00:11:47.639
That was what games were.

00:11:47.639 --> 00:11:52.404
Now 40 years later, we have
photo-realistic 3D simulations

00:11:52.404 --> 00:11:54.277
with millions of people
playing simultaneously

00:11:54.277 --> 00:11:56.218
and it's getting better every year.

00:11:56.218 --> 00:11:59.140
And soon we'll have virtual reality,

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:00.640
augmented reality.

00:12:01.846 --> 00:12:05.596
If you assume any rate
of improvement at all,

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:10.111
then the games will become
indistinguishable from reality.

00:12:10.111 --> 00:12:12.390
Indistinguishable.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:16.166
Even if that rate of
advancement drops by 1,000

00:12:16.166 --> 00:12:18.529
from what it is right now.

00:12:18.529 --> 00:12:20.471
Then you just say, okay,
well, let's imagine

00:12:20.471 --> 00:12:22.923
it's 10,000 years in the future.

00:12:22.923 --> 00:12:25.853
Which is nothing in
the evolutionary scale.

00:12:25.853 --> 00:12:26.686
So,

00:12:29.296 --> 00:12:31.529
so given that we're
clearly on our trajectory

00:12:31.529 --> 00:12:34.351
to have games that are
indistinguishable from reality

00:12:34.351 --> 00:12:37.368
and those games could be
played on any set top box

00:12:37.368 --> 00:12:39.337
or on a PC or whatever.

00:12:39.337 --> 00:12:42.920
And they would probably
be billions of such

00:12:45.388 --> 00:12:47.638
computers or set top boxes.

00:12:48.876 --> 00:12:52.448
It would seem to follow that the odds

00:12:52.448 --> 00:12:56.281
that we're in base reality
is one in billions.

00:12:57.314 --> 00:12:58.807
Tell me what's wrong with that argument?

00:12:58.807 --> 00:13:01.196
- Is the answer yes?

00:13:01.196 --> 00:13:02.645
- The argument is probably,

00:13:02.645 --> 00:13:05.017
is there a flow in that argument?

00:13:05.017 --> 00:13:07.521
I'm not sure what the error.

00:13:07.521 --> 00:13:08.670
- No, the argument makes sense.

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:11.263
So the assumption then is that

00:13:11.263 --> 00:13:14.792
somebody beat us to
it, and this is a game.

00:13:14.792 --> 00:13:18.369
- No, there's a one in billions
chance this is base reality.

00:13:18.369 --> 00:13:19.656
- Oh, okay.

00:13:19.656 --> 00:13:21.570
- What do you think?

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:22.905
- Well, I think it's one in billions.

00:13:22.905 --> 00:13:24.734
- Okay.
(audience laughs)

00:13:24.734 --> 00:13:25.683
- Yeah, I guess.

00:13:25.683 --> 00:13:28.537
- That seems to be like clearly

00:13:28.537 --> 00:13:29.954
what it suggests.

00:13:32.083 --> 00:13:33.873
And, actually, I mean,
arguably we should hope

00:13:33.873 --> 00:13:37.218
that that's true because otherwise

00:13:37.218 --> 00:13:40.013
if civilization stops advancing,

00:13:40.013 --> 00:13:42.209
then that may be due to
some calamitous event

00:13:42.209 --> 00:13:44.417
that erases civilization.

00:13:44.417 --> 00:13:47.329
So maybe we should be hopeful
that this is a simulation

00:13:47.329 --> 00:13:48.829
'cause otherwise--

00:13:50.204 --> 00:13:51.950
- 'Cause they could reboot it.

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:54.780
- Well, otherwise, either we're
going to create simulations

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.083
that are indistinguishable from reality

00:13:57.083 --> 00:13:59.322
or civilization will cease to exist.

00:13:59.322 --> 00:14:00.855
Those are the two options.

00:14:00.855 --> 00:14:02.017
(bell rings)

00:14:02.017 --> 00:14:03.268
- And fact number nine,

00:14:03.268 --> 00:14:08.060
he once made a guest experience
on the Big Bang Theory.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.150
- Here you go.

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:09.983
- Thanks.

00:14:11.311 --> 00:14:13.356
- You got to be kidding me.

00:14:13.356 --> 00:14:14.386
- Sorry?

00:14:14.386 --> 00:14:15.969
- You're Elon Musk.

00:14:17.082 --> 00:14:18.325
- I am.

00:14:18.325 --> 00:14:21.158
(audience laughs)

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.217
- What are you doing here?

00:14:26.217 --> 00:14:27.760
- I'm washing dishes.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:29.302
(audience laughs)

00:14:29.302 --> 00:14:30.545
Well, I was on the turkey line,

00:14:30.545 --> 00:14:32.783
but I got demoted for being
too generous with the gravy.

00:14:32.783 --> 00:14:35.756
(audience laughs)

00:14:35.756 --> 00:14:37.210
- Oh, man,

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.374
what an honor to meet you.

00:14:39.374 --> 00:14:42.374
I'm such a fan of Tesla and Space X,

00:14:44.222 --> 00:14:45.511
all your companies.

00:14:45.511 --> 00:14:47.829
Howard Wolowitz, Caltech.

00:14:47.829 --> 00:14:49.220
- Nice to meet you, Howard.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:50.230
It feels great to come down here

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:51.928
and help the less fortunate, huh?

00:14:51.928 --> 00:14:52.821
- Oh, yeah.

00:14:52.821 --> 00:14:54.910
(audience laughs)

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:58.151
Nothing better than helping people.

00:14:58.151 --> 00:15:00.096
(audience laughs)

00:15:00.096 --> 00:15:04.804
Which is something I realized
when I was viewing Earth

00:15:04.804 --> 00:15:08.427
from the deck of the
International Space Station,

00:15:08.427 --> 00:15:11.604
where I spent two months
as a payload specialist,

00:15:11.604 --> 00:15:13.961
a job I was qualified for because I'm

00:15:13.961 --> 00:15:15.716
an MIT trained engineer.

00:15:15.716 --> 00:15:17.797
(audience laughs)

00:15:17.797 --> 00:15:19.762
- And I thought I ladled
the gravy on thick.

00:15:19.762 --> 00:15:21.795
(audience laughs)

00:15:21.795 --> 00:15:24.190
- Sorry, it's just, you're you.

00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:26.945
(laughs) And I really
want you to adopt me.

00:15:26.945 --> 00:15:28.777
(audience laughs)

00:15:28.777 --> 00:15:30.066
- Well, you're here on Thanksgiving,

00:15:30.066 --> 00:15:31.312
so you're probably a good person.

00:15:31.312 --> 00:15:33.470
- Oh, I made my wife come down too.

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.748
(audience laughs)

00:15:35.748 --> 00:15:38.539
- You think you might ever
get back out into space?

00:15:38.539 --> 00:15:39.661
- Is that a job offer?

00:15:39.661 --> 00:15:41.884
Because I really want to go to Mars.

00:15:41.884 --> 00:15:43.232
Assuming I can bring my wife.

00:15:43.232 --> 00:15:44.575
She hardly takes up any room.

00:15:44.575 --> 00:15:45.746
She's basically a carry on.

00:15:45.746 --> 00:15:47.876
(audience laughs)

00:15:47.876 --> 00:15:48.912
- Well, we're not quite there yet.

00:15:48.912 --> 00:15:50.453
But we're always looking for engineers,

00:15:50.453 --> 00:15:53.022
so let me give you my
e-mail, we can stay in touch.

00:15:53.022 --> 00:15:54.022
- Thank you.

00:15:56.575 --> 00:15:57.574
- Oh, look.

00:15:57.574 --> 00:15:59.406
Someone hardly touched their pumpkin pie.

00:15:59.406 --> 00:16:00.449
Want to share it with me?

00:16:00.449 --> 00:16:03.282
(audience laughs)

00:16:04.136 --> 00:16:06.840
- A partially eaten piece of pumpkin pie

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:09.618
from a homeless shelter with Elon Musk?

00:16:09.618 --> 00:16:10.804
You bet I do.

00:16:10.804 --> 00:16:13.549
(audience laughs)

00:16:13.549 --> 00:16:14.464
- So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:16:14.464 --> 00:16:15.827
I hope you enjoyed.

00:16:15.827 --> 00:16:18.357
I'm super curious to figure
out which surprising fact

00:16:18.357 --> 00:16:21.990
was most interesting for
you, most surprising for you.

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:23.130
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.504
I'm going to check it out.

00:16:24.504 --> 00:16:25.685
Also, what do you think of this series?

00:16:25.685 --> 00:16:28.133
This is one of our series
that's on the bubble.

00:16:28.133 --> 00:16:31.103
We've done Tony Robbins,
we've done Bill Gates.

00:16:31.103 --> 00:16:32.857
We've done Kanye West.

00:16:32.857 --> 00:16:34.345
And this is Elon Musk.

00:16:34.345 --> 00:16:35.178
And we're not sure if we're going to

00:16:35.178 --> 00:16:36.495
continue the series or not,

00:16:36.495 --> 00:16:38.952
so I'd love to know what
did you like about it?

00:16:38.952 --> 00:16:39.865
Do you like it?

00:16:39.865 --> 00:16:41.025
How can we make it better?

00:16:41.025 --> 00:16:42.269
Share your thoughts down below.

00:16:42.269 --> 00:16:43.451
Maybe the series will continue

00:16:43.451 --> 00:16:46.115
or maybe we'll replace
it with something better.

00:16:46.115 --> 00:16:47.070
Let me know.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:48.274
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:16:48.274 --> 00:16:49.372
I believe in you.

00:16:49.372 --> 00:16:51.338
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:16:51.338 --> 00:16:53.800
and whatever your one word is.

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:55.002
Much love.

00:16:55.002 --> 00:16:56.669
I'll see you soon.

